ath12k: ahb: pick the memory profile from how much RAM there is

ath12k has two memory profiles. The default one sizes the firmware's
rings for a machine with memory to spare -- 17 vdevs, 32768 tx
descriptors, 12288 rx descriptors, 512 clients -- and the low profile
is roughly half that. ath12k_core_get_memory_mode() chooses between
them by looking at totalram.

The PCIe path calls it. The AHB path hardcodes the default:

  ab->target_mem_mode = ATH12K_QMI_MEMORY_MODE_DEFAULT;

so every AHB board tells its firmware, over QMI host_cap, that it has
memory to spare regardless of the truth. On IPQ5332 with 512 MiB and
about 48 MiB of that reserved for the Q6, totalram lands below the
threshold and the low profile is the correct answer.

The vendor device tree says as much in its own terms -- it carries
qcom,tgt-mem-mode = <2> on the wifi node rather than leaving it at the
default.

Call the same helper the PCIe path does.

Signed-off-by: Hideo Sumi <hideo.sumi@mugops.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
@@ -1022,7 +1022,15 @@
 	ab->hif.ops = hif_ops;
 	ab->pdev = pdev;
 	ab->hw_rev = hw_rev;
-	ab->target_mem_mode = ATH12K_QMI_MEMORY_MODE_DEFAULT;
+	/* Pick the memory profile from how much RAM Linux actually has,
+	 * the way the PCIe path already does. Hardcoding the default
+	 * profile tells the firmware to size its rings for a machine with
+	 * memory to spare -- 17 vdevs, 32768 tx descriptors, 12288 rx
+	 * descriptors -- which is not this one: 512 MiB with roughly 48 MiB
+	 * of it carved out for the Q6 leaves totalram below the threshold
+	 * that selects the low-memory profile.
+	 */
+	ab->target_mem_mode = ath12k_core_get_memory_mode(ab);
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, ab);
 	ab_ahb = ath12k_ab_to_ahb(ab);
 	ab_ahb->ab = ab;
